    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 231757\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1057 AM PDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above normal temperatures continue through next week.\n\n* Isolated storm chances linger into early next week, with best \n  chances in northwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Saturday.\n\nWith a fairly persistent pattern expected, temperatures remain\nabove normal through the forecast period. Ensemble guidance \nkeeps the 500mb ridge anchored in southern NM and southeastern AZ,\nleaving us under south-southwest flow aloft. A subtle cooldown is\nexpected Monday-Tuesday as moisture increases in our southeastern\nareas, but excessive heat quickly returns by mid-week and sticks \naround. Elevated low temperatures will offer little-to-no \novernight relief, especially in the Mojave Desert. Major to \nextreme heatrisk is forecast to return to these areas during the \nsecond half of the week.\n\nThe aforementioned south-southwest flow is just southerly enough\nto let moisture linger but southwesterly enough to keep it \nlimited. These conditions should favor isolated afternoon \nconvection, primarily in northwestern AZ and the southern Great \nBasin where odds are 10-30%. Main concerns with this activity are \ngusty outflow winds and dry lightning, though brief heavy \ndownpours are possible. An influx of moisture on Monday may result\nin an uptick in coverage along/southeast of I-15, but models \nstill disagree on the northwestward extent and magnitude of this \ninflux. During the second half of the week, the flow acquires a \ngreater westerly component, allowing moisture and precipitation \nchances to subside.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nfavor a southeasterly direction this morning with sustained speeds \nof less than 10 knots. A few gusts to around 15 knots cannot be \nruled out. In the early afternoon, winds will shift to the southwest \nwith gusts of around 20 knots. Winds will decrease but maintain a \nsouthwesterly direction after sunset. Temperatures exceed 100 \ndegrees until 04Z this evening, then increase to above 100 degrees \nby 18Z tomorrow.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Southerly winds with \ngusts between 20 and 25 knots develop this afternoon in the Las \nVegas Valley, Colorado River Valley, and Owens Valley, weakening \nafter sunset. Gusty westerly winds pick up after sunset at KDAG, \nlasting into the early morning hours. Thunderstorms will continue to \ndrift through Mohave County this afternoon, and additional storm \ndevelopment cannot be ruled out over high terrain in southern Nevada \nas well. Gusty outflow winds may cause sudden wind direction changes \nin the Las Vegas Valley and Colorado River Valley. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
